non-expert's agency, and the evidence of this is the 'expert-checklist' performance dip.","counterconditions":["The expert's intuition is fundamentally undisciplined and the checklist provides necessary guardrails.","The task is purely procedural with no 'higher' synthesis required."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"If providing the expert with the same checklist reduces their reliability, it proves the checklist is a constraint rather than a tool. The panel's 'reliability' is merely the ceiling of the checklist; if the expert's intuition exceeds that ceiling, the panel is objectively less reliable in capturing the full truth of the verifiable outcome.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.2}],"scratch_proposal":{"cluster_suggestions":[],"links":[],"new_blocks":[{"body":{"content":"What if the 'evidence' isn't in the answer, but in the 'disagreement log'? If the panel reaches the right answer through a series of internal contradictions and corrections (a 'noisy' path), it suggests genuine reliability. If they reach the right answer with 100% silent agreement on every checklist item, it suggests mere compliance/blind-ticking.","why_keep_this":"Proposes a meta-metric for the 'evidence' part of the prompt."},"local_key":"NEW_001"}],"revisions":[],"unresolved_questions":[]},"simulation_proposals":[]}